# scaleup_single_switch_144_3600Gbps.topo
#
# Models an NVLink-class SCALE-UP domain as a single non-blocking crossbar:
# 144 hosts (GPUs) all attached to ONE ToR switch, every pair exactly one hop apart.
# Per-host link = 3600 Gbps (NVLink-aggregate class); switch latency 300 ns, link 500 ns.
#
# HARDWARE CAVEAT: 144 GPUs exceeds the current single-tier NVLink domain (NVL72). It
# corresponds to the announced Rubin-Ultra "NVL576" rack (144 GPU packages / 576 dies).
# At that scale NVIDIA's own scale-up fabric is NO LONGER a single non-blocking crossbar:
# it becomes a TWO-TIER, multi-plane (possibly oversubscribed / non-Clos) NVSwitch
# network, and a single NVSwitch chip's radix (~64) is exceeded. This single-switch model
# is therefore an IDEALISED upper bound ("fantasise about a very large single domain",
# supervisor 2026-07-13) that isolates the collective's scaling on one hypothetical
# crossbar; it does not model the real two-tier fabric, its extra hop, or finite switch
# radix. Use it to show the N-scaling trend, not as a faithful NVL576 hardware model.
#
# Encoding note: htsim's FatTree parser rejects Tiers=1 (fat_tree_topology.cpp), so a
# single switch is expressed as a DEGENERATE 2-tier fat-tree: Podsize == Nodes and
# Tier-0 Radix_Down == Nodes => exactly one ToR holds all hosts. The Tier-1 uplink
# (Radix_Up 1) is vestigial and carries no host<->host traffic (all pairs are same-ToR,
# one hop). Oversubscribed == Nodes keeps radix_down/radix_up == oversub (parser check).
# NOTE: 144 is NOT a power of two, so recursive-doubling is skipped for this N.
Nodes 144
Tiers 2
Podsize 144

Tier 0
Downlink_speed_Gbps 3600
Radix_Down 144
Radix_Up 1
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
Oversubscribed 144

Tier 1
Downlink_speed_Gbps 3600
Radix_Down 1
Downlink_Latency_ns 500
Switch_Latency_ns 300
